--- !u!114 &5200000000000001003
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 5200000000000001001}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 0cdea111003b42c8a90f31d5b6e7c903, type: 3}
  m_Name:
  m_EditorClassIdentifier:
  name: Sequence Order
  minigameType: 81
  instructionsLabel: Rebuild the sequence in the correct order
  resultOnTimeout: 0
  standardDuration: 120
  minigameUIPrefab: {fileID: 6200000000000001003, guid: 0cdea111011b42c8a90f31d5b6e7c911, type: 3}
  descriptionImage: {fileID: 0}
  descriptionHeader: Sequence Order
  description: 'The briefing names a security sequence you must know by heart. The
    terms on the left are SCRAMBLED - click one to drop it into the lowest free slot
    on the right, and click a placed term to take it back. When every slot is filled,
    press CHECK ORDER - correct positions LOCK in green, wrong ones bounce back to
    the pool. Every failed check burns one of your limited checks - run out and the
    console locks. Placing and rearranging is free; checking is not.'
  scenarios:
  - scenarioName: Data encapsulation
    recommendedDifficulty: 1
    headline: Order the encapsulation stages of outgoing data - from the application
      payload down to the signals on the wire.
    items:
    - DATA
    - SEGMENT
    - PACKET
    - FRAME
    - BITS
    lesson: Each layer wraps the payload from the layer above with its own header
      - the name of the wrapped unit (PDU) tells you which layer you are looking
      at.
  - scenarioName: DHCP lease (DORA)
    recommendedDifficulty: 1
    headline: Order the DHCP messages a new client exchanges with the server to obtain
      an IP address.
    items:
    - DISCOVER
    - OFFER
    - REQUEST
    - ACKNOWLEDGE
    lesson: DORA - Discover, Offer, Request, Acknowledge. A rogue device can answer
      the broadcast Discover too, which is why rogue-DHCP detection matters.
  - scenarioName: OSI model
    recommendedDifficulty: 2
    headline: Stack the OSI model from Layer 1 in slot 1 (the wire) up to Layer 7
      in slot 7 (the user application).
    items:
    - PHYSICAL
    - DATA LINK
    - NETWORK
    - TRANSPORT
    - SESSION
    - PRESENTATION
    - APPLICATION
    lesson: Please Do Not Throw Sausage Pizza Away - Physical, Data Link, Network,
      Transport, Session, Presentation, Application.
  - scenarioName: Cyber Kill Chain
    recommendedDifficulty: 2
    headline: Reconstruct the Cyber Kill Chain - from the attacker's first step to
      the final impact.
    items:
    - RECONNAISSANCE
    - WEAPONIZATION
    - DELIVERY
    - EXPLOITATION
    - INSTALLATION
    - 'COMMAND & CONTROL'
    - ACTIONS ON OBJECTIVES
    lesson: Breaking ANY single link stops the whole chain - the defender only needs
      to win at one stage, the attacker must win them all.
  - scenarioName: Incident response
    recommendedDifficulty: 2
    headline: Order the incident-response lifecycle a SOC follows once an intrusion
      is confirmed.
    items:
    - PREPARATION
    - 'DETECTION & ANALYSIS'
    - CONTAINMENT
    - ERADICATION
    - RECOVERY
    - LESSONS LEARNED
    lesson: Containment comes BEFORE eradication - cut the attacker off first, clean
      up second, and feed lessons learned back into preparation.
  - scenarioName: Digital forensics
    recommendedDifficulty: 3
    headline: Order the digital-forensics process - from the first sighting of evidence
      to the courtroom.
    items:
    - IDENTIFICATION
    - PRESERVATION
    - COLLECTION
    - EXAMINATION
    - ANALYSIS
    - PRESENTATION
    lesson: Preserve before you collect and analyse - evidence handled out of order
      breaks the chain of custody and dies in court.
  checksAllowed: 3
  pointsPerCorrectSlot: 100
  failedCheckPenalty: 75
  solveBonus: 300
  timeBonusPerSecond: 5
  bonusPerCheckLeft: 100
